passeth:
From which they lately had their birth;
Sweet life, but sweeter death that passeth by
And is as though it had not been:--

passing:
Come to the pane, draw the curtain apart,
There she is passing, the girl of my heart;
See where she walks like a queen in the street,

passengers:
The veriest slave who drops in Cairo’s street,
Cries, ‘Stand off from me,’ to the passengers;
While these blotched souls are eager to infect,

passion:
Their warm, heart-melting motions to be wooed;
And passion boldly rising in my breast,
Like rivers of the Spring, lets loose its flood.

passionately:
He thought it should be so too, with his love;
He, passionately loving, would bring down
His love, his life, his best, (because the best)

passages:
And waving tapestries that argue forth
Strange passages into the outer air;
So in this dimmer room which we call life,

passenger:
For bags and wraps the red-caps circle round;
From off the step the passenger lightly hops,
And seeks his cab or tram-car homeward bound;

pastures:
Fed full of noises by invisible streams;
And open pastures, where you scarcely tell
White daisies from white dew,—at intervals

With honeyed milk and warm, white bread;
His ways in pastures green are led,
And perfumed oil illumes his head;

passion's:
When ghostly lanes turn toward the shadowy morn;
When bodies weighed with satiate passion's power
Sad, disappointed from each other turn;

passin:
D' ain't a hymn ner co'n-song ringin' in de air;
But de murmur of a branch's passin' waters
Is de only soun' dat breks de stillness dere.

An' dat Tildy, umph! she giggled, an' she gin huh dress a flirt
Lak de people she was passin' was ez common ez de dirt;
